<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bishopric of Semigallia</h1><p class="st-meta">1218 CE – 1251 CE (approx.) · capital Selburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived missionary bishopric for the unconquered Semigallians, created in 1218 and merged into the see of Riga in 1251.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1218 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert of Riga</a> obtained permission to create a new diocese for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/semigallians">Semigallia</a>, still largely pagan. Bernhard zur Lippe, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobleman turned Cistercian abbot, became its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> resisted, the bishops based themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selpils">Selburg</a> (Sēlpils) in the land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selonians">Selonians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The bishopric existed mainly on paper, since most of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/semigallians">Semigallia</a> remained independent. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> controlled a few districts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>, depending on support from Riga and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>. A papal legate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-of-alna">Baldwin of Alna</a>, briefly tried to reorganise the see in the 1230s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1251 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> united the diocese with Riga, which shortly afterwards became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/semigallians">Semigallia</a> itself was conquered only in 1290, and its land was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and the archbishop.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1218 CE – 1251 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.94%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1218 CE – 1251 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Selburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernhard zur Lippe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Middle Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bishopric of Selonia, Bishopric of Selonia-Semigallia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1234&amp;focus=bishopric-of-semigallia" aria-label="Bishopric of Semigallia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5224 246 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bishopric of Semigallia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 502h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5544 502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Latvia. <a href="/atlas?year=1234&amp;focus=bishopric-of-semigallia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
